          </table></div>
          <p>
            A few of these need context. <strong>Life Fitness</strong> runs two stores. The facility
            store, which requires a business account, listed the Integrity+ treadmill with the SL console
            at $11,247 with a nine-week ship time. The consumer store sells the same family of machines
            to home buyers and, when checked, had new "outlet" Integrity units at $5,940 with the SL
            console (regular $7,425) and $7,183 with the SE3 HD touchscreen (regular $13,059), with free
            delivery and installation. Outlet stock comes and goes, so treat those as what a good day
            looks like rather than a standing price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Star Trac's</strong> 8 Series is a common sight in US gyms. Fitness Superstore listed a
            new 8 Series TR with the LED console at $9,199 with the full manufacturer's warranty; The Fitness Outlet, which describes
            itself as an authorised Star Trac dealer, listed the 8 Series TRx with a touchscreen at $10,099
            against a regular $11,299. <strong>True</strong> does not publish prices on its own site, but
            Schellers listed the TC900 at $10,099 to $13,399 across four console options, with a five-year
            motor, three-year parts and three-year labour warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Woodway</strong> is the outlier. Its 4Front uses a slatted belt on ball bearings rather
            than a belt over a deck, and Woodway's own shop listed it at $13,995 with a warranty of five
            years on the drive, motor and belt, three years on all components and one year on labour,
            though the listing showed as sold out when checked. <strong>Technogym's</strong> US prices are
            covered in our <a href="/technogym-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Technogym</a> guide, which recorded the Excite+ Run from $10,460 on Technogym's site
            on 20 October 2026; Technogym's club-only lines go considerably higher.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Precor and Matrix</strong> are the two big gym brands that mostly sell new commercial
            treadmills by quote. Precor's US product page for the TRM 835 lists a 4 HP AC drive motor and
            a 400 lb user limit but offers only a sales contact form, and the Matrix dealer pages we could
            load either said "call for pricing" or were remanufactured stock. If you need a new price from
            either, ask an authorised dealer and get the quote in writing with delivery, installation and
            warranty spelled out; the next section explains why that quote may differ from anything you
            see online.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="light-vs-full" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Light-Commercial vs. Full-Commercial: What the Price Gap Buys</h2>
<p>
            "Commercial" is not one grade. Makers and dealers use at least two, and the price gap between
            them is large, so it is worth knowing which one a quote or listing refers to.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Full-commercial</strong> treadmills are rated for the heaviest duty: health clubs and
            large gyms where a machine may run from opening to closing under users of every weight and
            gait. These are the $9,000 to $14,000 machines in the table above. They typically use AC drive
            motors, heavy welded steel frames, large rollers and decks designed to be flipped or replaced,
            and they come with commercial warranty terms that assume many hours of use a day. They also
            tend to have electrical requirements a home may not meet; many call for a dedicated circuit,
            and the installation guide for the specific model is the document to check before you buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Light-commercial</strong> treadmills are built for lighter institutional duty: hotel
            gyms, apartment and condo fitness rooms, small studios, physical therapy clinics and offices,
            where a machine sees a few users a day rather than a queue. They usually share some
            engineering with the maker's club line but with a lighter frame, a smaller motor or a simpler
            console, and they sit between the premium home tier and the full-commercial tier in price.
            Many makers sell the same or similar light-commercial machines to home buyers, which is why
            you will see "commercial-grade" on home treadmills around $3,500 to $6,000.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical differences that justify the higher price are duty rating, serviceability and
            warranty. A full-commercial machine is designed so that a technician can <a href="/how-to-replace-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>replace the belt</a>,
            deck, rollers, motor and control board quickly, because a gym loses money every day a
            treadmill is out of order. A light-commercial or home machine may use the same kind of parts,
            but it is not usually built or warranted for the same number of hours. If you are buying for
            a hotel or apartment gym, read the warranty for the words that define "commercial use" and
            "light commercial use"; some makers cover the same machine for different periods depending on
            where it is installed.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a home, this distinction matters less than it seems. A household with two or three
            runners does not put a treadmill through anything like club duty, so a light-commercial or
            premium home machine usually covers it. Our guide to the <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best commercial-grade treadmills for home</a> looks at the machines that sit at this boundary and what the label does and does not guarantee.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="quote-pricing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Why So Many Commercial Prices Are "Call for Quote"</h2>
<p>
            Home treadmills are sold like any other consumer product: a list price, a sale price and a
            checkout button. Commercial treadmills are sold more like vehicles to a fleet. The listed
            price, where there is one, is a starting point, and three things move it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Configuration.</strong> The same base can carry very different consoles. True's TC900
            spans $3,300 between its cheapest and most expensive console at one dealer, and Life Fitness's
            outlet prices differed by more than $1,200 between the SL and SE3 HD consoles on the same day.
            A touchscreen console with entertainment and connectivity can account for a large share of the
            price, and it is also the part most likely to look dated in five years. For a gym, the
            decision usually comes down to whether members expect a screen; for a home, a simpler console
            is often the better use of the money.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Volume and package deals.</strong> A gym fitting out a cardio floor may buy ten or
            twenty treadmills alongside bikes, ellipticals and strength equipment. Dealers and makers
            price those packages individually, which is the main reason so many commercial brands do not
            publish a single unit price. A single home buyer should expect to pay closer to the listed
            figure than a club ordering a full floor.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What is included.</strong> One quote may include freight, installation, removal of
            old equipment and a service plan; another may be the machine on a pallet at the kerb. Before
            comparing two quotes, write each one out as the same list: machine and console, delivery
            method, installation, warranty terms in years for parts and labour, any service plan, and
            tax. Only then are the totals comparable. The same applies to a used or remanufactured
            machine, where delivery and installation are often extras.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used-remanufactured" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Used, Refurbished and Remanufactured Commercial Treadmill Prices</h2>
<p>
            Gyms replace cardio equipment on a cycle, so the second-hand market for commercial
            treadmills is large, and it is where most home buyers who want a gym machine end up. The
            words sellers use matter, because they describe very different amounts of work.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Used or "as is":</strong> the machine as it came out of the gym, perhaps cleaned
            and tested. Cheapest, with little or no warranty.</li>
            <li><strong>Refurbished:</strong> cleaned, inspected and repaired where needed, often with
            new wear parts such as the belt. The term has no fixed definition, so ask what was replaced.</li>
            <li><strong>Remanufactured:</strong> usually the most thorough process, with the machine
            stripped, worn parts replaced and the frame often repainted, sold with a written warranty.
            Again, the seller's own description of the process is what counts.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            These are the remanufactured prices listed by large US resellers when checked in November
            2026. Both resellers ran Black Friday codes at the time that took a further 5 to 10% off.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Seller</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Listed price</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Warranty stated</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Precor TRM 835 V2, P30 console</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Build A Gym</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,495</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year parts and labour</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Precor TRM 835 V2, P30 console</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fitness Superstore</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,999</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year parts and on-site labour</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Matrix T3x</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fitness Superstore</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,799</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year parts and on-site labour</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Woodway 4Front, Quick Set display</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fitness Superstore</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$9,599</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year parts and on-site labour</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two things stand out. First, the same model can differ by $1,500 between sellers, as the two
            Precor TRM 835 listings show, so compare what each includes before assuming the cheaper one is
            the better deal: shipping, the delivery method, the warranty and the return terms all varied.
            Second, the discount from new is far smaller for slat-belt machines. A remanufactured <a href="/woodway-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Woodway</a> 4Front at $9,599 is about 31% below Woodway's $13,995 new listing, while a remanufactured belt
            treadmill commonly sells for well under half of a comparable new club machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            Private sales and gym liquidations go lower than any of these, but with no warranty and a
            machine you will have to move yourself, often weighing several hundred pounds. If you go that
            route, check the hours or distance on the console, the belt and deck, the motor under load and
            the incline through its full range; our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill buying checklist</a> goes through each test. For what separates a good reseller from a poor one, and what a
            refurbishment should include, see our guide to <a href="/refurbished-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">refurbished treadmills</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="leasing-financing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Leasing and Financing Commercial Treadmills for a Gym</h2>
<p>
            Most gyms do not pay cash for a full cardio floor. Commercial equipment is commonly leased or
            financed, either through the maker's own finance arm, a dealer's finance partner or an
            independent equipment lender. The rates and terms depend on the business's credit and the
            lender, so there is no honest standard figure to quote, but the structures are worth knowing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Equipment finance agreements</strong> work like a loan secured on the equipment: the
            gym makes fixed payments and owns the machines at the end. <strong>Leases</strong> come in two
            broad kinds. A lease with a nominal buyout at the end (often called a $1 buyout lease) behaves
            much like a loan, and the gym ends up owning the equipment. A fair-market-value lease has lower
            payments, and at the end the gym can return the equipment, renew, or buy it at its then market
            value. That second kind suits a gym that plans to refresh its cardio floor every few years
            anyway, because the old machines go back rather than needing to be sold.
          </p>
          <p>
            When comparing offers, ask for the total of all payments plus any buyout, not just the
            monthly figure; ask whether delivery, installation and a service plan are rolled into the
            financed amount; ask what happens if a machine fails during the term; and ask what the
            end-of-term options cost. The tax treatment of a lease versus a purchase differs, and
            equipment purchases may qualify for depreciation deductions, but that depends on the business,
            so the question belongs with an accountant rather than a salesperson.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a home buyer, commercial leasing is rarely available or sensible. Home buyers financing
            a commercial-grade machine are usually offered the same consumer plans as any other treadmill:
            0% promotions, deferred-interest store cards and buy-now-pay-later. Those carry the same
            traps as on a cheaper machine, chief among them <a href="/treadmill-financing/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>deferred interest</a> charged back to the purchase
            date if the balance is not cleared in time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="service-delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Service Contracts, Warranty and Delivery Costs</h2>
<p>
            The machine is not the whole bill. With commercial treadmills the extras are larger than on
            home machines, because the equipment is heavier and the cost of downtime is higher.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Delivery and installation.</strong> Commercial treadmills are heavy and often ship
            mostly assembled, so getting one into a room is a job for a crew. Fitness Superstore, for
            example, listed curbside delivery free on orders over $4,000 in the contiguous US, with upgrades
            to garage delivery at $100, garage installation at $349, ground-level room-of-choice
            installation at $449 to $499 depending on the machine, and a stair carry at $599 to $699
            (checked November 2026). Life Fitness's consumer store included delivery and installation on
            its outlet Integrity treadmills. Stairs, tight turns and basement doors can add to the price
            or rule a machine out, so measure the route before ordering.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Warranty.</strong> New commercial warranties vary by maker and by whether the machine
            is installed in a home or a facility. Examples from listings checked in November 2026: Woodway
            listed five years on the 4Front's drive, motor and belt, three years on all components and one
            year on labour; Schellers listed five years on the motor and three years on parts and labour
            for the True TC900; and Life Fitness's consumer store listed lifetime frame cover and one year of
            labour on its outlet Integrity units. Remanufactured machines typically came with one year of
            parts and labour.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Extended service plans.</strong> Resellers sell longer cover separately. Fitness
            Superstore's options for the remanufactured Matrix T3x ran from $299 for two years of
            residential cover to $1,749 for five years of commercial cover, and Build A Gym offered an extra
            year on the remanufactured Precor TRM 835 for $399 or two extra years for $749. For a gym, a
            preventive maintenance contract with a local service company, covering regular cleaning,
            belt and deck checks and lubrication where the design calls for it, is often the better use
            of money than an extended warranty alone, because it reduces how often machines fail at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Running costs.</strong> Wear parts are the main recurring cost on a heavily used
            commercial treadmill. Belts and decks wear with mileage, and a full-commercial machine in a
            busy gym covers far more distance in a year than any home treadmill. Ask the dealer what a <a href="/best-treadmill-replacement-belts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>replacement belt</a> and deck cost for the model you are quoting, since those prices vary a great
            deal by brand and you will likely pay them at least once over the machine's life.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="total-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Adding It Up: Two Worked Examples</h2>
<p>
            Prices on their own are hard to compare, so here are two illustrative totals built only from
            the listed figures above. Tax, local freight differences and promotions will change them; the
            point is the shape of the bill, not the exact number.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Line item</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">New: Star Trac 8 Series TR</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Remanufactured: Matrix T3x</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Machine</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$9,199</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,799</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Curbside delivery</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$0 (over $4,000)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$0 (curbside included)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Delivery upgrades, garage to room of choice</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Up to $549 for a stair carry</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$100 to $599</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Extra warranty</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Maker's commercial warranty included</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$299 to $1,749 depending on term</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Rough total before tax</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $9,200 to $9,750</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $3,800 to $6,150</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The pattern holds across most comparisons. A remanufactured machine roughly halves the cost of
            getting a club-grade frame and motor into a room, but the gap narrows once you add a longer
            service plan, because the new machine arrives with the maker's full warranty and the
            remanufactured one usually with a year. For a gym, the comparison also has to include how
            long the machine will stay on the floor before it is replaced; for a home, where use is light
            by commercial standards, a remanufactured machine with a year of cover and a good reseller
            behind it is often the better value.
          </p>
          <p>
            The ranges are wide on purpose. Each runs from the cheapest option the reseller listed,
            curbside delivery and the included warranty, to the most expensive, a stair carry into the
            room of choice and, for the remanufactured machine, the longest commercial service plan. Most
            buyers land somewhere between, depending on their stairs and how long they want cover.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-it-at-home" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is a Commercial Treadmill Worth It at Home?</h2>
<p>
            For most homes, a new full-commercial treadmill is more machine than the use calls for. A
            household running a few hours a week does not come close to the duty a gym treadmill is built
            for, and the extra money mostly buys durability you will not use, a footprint you have to
            find room for, and in many cases an electrical requirement your house may not meet without an
            electrician.
          </p>
          <p>
            There are real exceptions. Several heavy runners sharing one machine, very high weekly
            mileage, a heavier user who wants the stiffness of a club frame, or a buyer who would rather
            pay once for a machine that will outlast them can all justify it. Even then, the usual sweet
            spot is not a new club unit but a remanufactured one from a reseller who stands behind it, or a
            commercial maker's home line. Our guide to bringing a <a href="/gym-treadmill-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym treadmill into a home</a> covers the practical side: the doorways, the weight, the plug and the floor.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before you buy, it helps to know which brands gyms actually run, because those are the
            machines most often available remanufactured and the ones local technicians know how to fix.
            Our guide to <a href="/what-treadmills-do-gyms-use/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what treadmills gyms use</a> covers the main commercial brands and who owns them. And if what you really want is a home
            treadmill built to a high standard rather than a gym machine as such, the <a href="/treadmills-over-2500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills over $2,500</a> include several premium and light-commercial home machines that cost less than a new club unit
            and need less of your house to accommodate them.
          </p>
          <p>
            A simple test: if you would not notice the difference between a premium home treadmill and a
            gym treadmill on your normal workout, the commercial premium is buying you longevity and
            nothing else. Longevity is worth paying for when the machine is used hard. When it is not, the
            money is better spent on a well-built home machine and a longer warranty.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Where People Go Wrong When Pricing a Commercial Treadmill</h2>
          <p>Most bad commercial treadmill purchases come from comparing the wrong numbers or skipping a question. These are the four that cost the most.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Comparing quotes that include different things</h3>
              <p>One quote includes installation and a service plan, another is the machine at the kerb. Write every quote out as the same list, machine and console, delivery, installation, warranty in years for parts and labour, and tax, before deciding which is cheaper.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying for a console nobody will use</h3>
              <p>The touchscreen can be the biggest single difference between two prices for the same base, as True's $10,099 to $13,399 TC900 range shows. For a home or a small facility, a simpler console usually does the job and is less likely to look dated or fail first.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying "refurbished" without asking what was done</h3>
              <p>The word has no fixed meaning. Ask which wear parts were replaced, get the warranty in writing, and check who honours it. A cheaper listing with no stated process and no on-site labour cover can cost more in the first repair than it saved.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring the route and the circuit</h3>
              <p>A commercial treadmill can weigh several hundred pounds and ship mostly assembled. Measure doorways, stairs and turns, and check the model's installation guide for its electrical requirement before ordering, not on delivery day.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">New full-commercial treadmills</strong> were listed at roughly $9,000
            to $14,000 in November 2026, from Star Trac's 8 Series TR at $9,199 to Woodway's 4Front at
            $13,995, with Precor and Matrix mostly sold by quote. Light-commercial and outlet machines
            sold to homes start lower, and remanufactured gym belt treadmills run about $2,500 to $4,000.
          </p>
          <p>
            Budget for <strong class="text-white">delivery, installation and a service plan</strong>, compare
            quotes line by line, and for a home, ask whether a remanufactured or premium home machine does
            the same job for less. For the full range of treadmill prices below the commercial tier, see <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">how much a treadmill costs</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

