        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How much does a commercial treadmill cost?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              New full-commercial treadmills from gym brands were listed at roughly $9,000 to $14,000 at US dealers in November 2026, for example Star Trac's 8 Series TR at $9,199 and Woodway's 4Front at $13,995. Remanufactured ex-gym belt treadmills were listed from about $2,500 to $4,000. Delivery, installation, tax and any service plan come on top.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much is a used commercial treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Remanufactured units with a one-year warranty were listed at about $2,500 to $4,000 for belt treadmills such as the Precor TRM 835 and Matrix T3x in November 2026. Private sales and gym liquidations can be cheaper but usually come with no warranty and no delivery. Slat-belt machines hold their value better; a remanufactured Woodway 4Front was $9,599.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much is a Life Fitness commercial treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Life Fitness's facility store listed the Integrity+ treadmill with the SL console at $11,247 in November 2026, for business-account buyers. Its consumer store had new outlet Integrity units for home buyers at $5,940 and $7,183 depending on console, with delivery and installation included. Outlet stock changes, so check the store on the day.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why do commercial treadmills cost so much more than home treadmills?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              They are built and warranted for many hours a day under many users, which takes heavier frames, AC drive motors, larger rollers and decks designed for replacement. They are also designed to be serviced quickly, and the price includes a dealer and service network. Large touchscreen consoles add a significant amount on top.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you lease a commercial treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Businesses usually can, through the maker's finance arm, a dealer's partner or an equipment lender. The main choices are a finance agreement or a $1 buyout lease, where you own the machine at the end, and a fair-market-value lease, where you can return it. Compare the total of all payments and the end-of-term costs, and ask an accountant about the tax treatment.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a commercial treadmill worth it for home use?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually not new, because home use rarely approaches commercial duty. It can be worth it for several heavy runners sharing one machine or very high mileage, and a remanufactured gym treadmill from a reputable reseller is often the better-value way to get one. Check the doorways, floor and electrical circuit first.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
